condition_name: Asphyxia in Neonates
condition_slug: asphyxia-in-neonates
source: NSTG 2022
introduction: "Asphyxia in neonates can lead to hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy, affecting various bodily functions depending on severity."
clinical_features[1]:
  - type: Clinical Manifestations
    features[9]: Inability to cry or suck,Global hypotonia and poor activity,Global hypertonia with brisk deep tendon reflexes,Shock and oliguria,Poor temperature regulation,Paralytic ileus,Respiratory distress,Bleeding tendencies,Seizures
investigations[0]:
treatment:
  goals[0]:
  non_drug[5]: "Serial monitoring of parameters (oxygen saturation via pulse oximetry, random blood glucose, serum electrolytes, urea and creatinine, serum calcium, serum troponin, serum aspartate aminotransferase, serum alanine aminotransferase where available)",Apnea management with frequent airway clearing and ventilation by bag and mask,"Nil per oral until peristalsis is present and respiratory rate is normal, feeding with expressed breast milk via nasogastric or orogastric tube","Monitor urine output, weigh diaper or catheterize for adequate monitoring, and consult nephrologist if oliguria noted",Selective head cooling (if available)
  drug[4]: Slow intravenous phenobarbitone for seizures,Use of inotropes like dobutamine or dopamine for hypotension not corrected by fluid bolus,Magnesium sulphate to improve outcome,Oxygen administration to maintain SpO₂ >94%
  adverse_reactions_and_cautions[2]: Prophylactic phenobarbitone is no longer standard practice,Fluid restriction in perinatal asphyxia management is no longer favored
  supportive_measures[5]: Derangements corrected according to standard protocols,"Prevent over-hydration, administer exact daily maintenance fluid requirement",Use fluid bolus of normal saline in aliquots if circulatory collapse occurs,Correct electrolyte derangement as needed,Treat hypoglycemia as per serial serum monitoring
differential_diagnoses[0]:
complications[0]:
prevention[0]:
other_investigations: null
definitive_treatment: null
prognosis[1]: Persistence of abnormal cry and motor functions beyond first week suggests severe cerebral damage